From 8c3b95f73c6d2386dce90bac4d8541389e6a9617 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Paul Schmitt <paul.schmitt@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 16:34:58 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] launcher: lay the app list out on a clock face

The first app sits at the 12 o'clock position, the second at 1 o'clock and
so on clockwise; Up and Down step by one hour position. Every app keeps a
fixed, memorable place on the dial rather than one that depends on how far
you have scrolled, and one step is exactly one crown detent.

Behind CONFIG_LAUNCHER_RADIAL, so the stock list launcher stays buildable
and the two can be compared. radial_menu_layer.c implements the same
launcher_menu_layer_* API, so launcher.c is untouched and shared; the
launcher sources are globbed, so menu_layer.c is guarded to compile only
when the option is off.

Drawing is flat black on white with no animation, aimed at a panel that
redraws slowly, where a moving selection costs a refresh. There are no app
glances for the same reason. Details that came out of running it:

- No tick marks. The app dots already mark the hour positions, so ticks
  were clutter and another thing to redraw.
- The centre label steps down through font sizes and then ellipsizes,
  rather than wrapping. Word-wrap broke a long name mid-syllable
  ("Notification-s").
- Auto-repeat is 400 ms, slower than a slow panel's refresh. A faster
  repeat turns one deliberate press into two steps and overshoots the app
  you meant.
- Apps past the first twelve wrap onto an inner ring instead of piling
  onto the outer one.

Firmware is slightly smaller than with the list launcher (1798232 vs
1807000 bytes) since the MenuLayer machinery drops out.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
